I've written the plan for Day 264: two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The prescribed command ends in || true, so it expects that, and I didn't force-add. The files are on disk for the implementation phase.

Task 1: Part of #1216 (product, data loss). If .yoyo/memory.json doesn't parse, yoyo reads it as an empty store, and the next /remember overwrites it. The assessment reproduced this on the built binary: one trailing comma, then /remember new note, and every earlier memory was gone with no warning. The task:
• Anything that loads the file, changes it and saves it back must refuse when the file doesn't parse. That covers /remember, auto-remember and eviction. The message names the file, the line and column, and how to fix it.
• Code that only displays memories or puts them in the prompt warns once instead of silently treating the file as empty.
• Tests pin that the file's bytes don't change. They include a no-typo run that must still work and a deliberate-break check, both posted to #1216 as soon as they run.
• A census covers every other store yoyo loads, changes and saves. Any broken one it finds gets its own ticket.
• src/memory.rs is already 1,987 lines against the 2,000-line cap, so new tests go in a separate file.

The title stays "Part of" because of #1215: the harness copies the plan's "Fixes" onto leftover commits and closes tickets early.

Task 2: Release v0.5.0 (product). The release is due: 15 days and 50 commits since v0.4.0, including the security, overwrite and safety fixes. The task follows skills/release/SKILL.md:
• Run all the checks, including both price audits. Any price drift gets noted on #1012.
• Finish the CHANGELOG and bump the version to 0.5.0.
• Push the v0.5.0 tag and confirm it reached origin. Don't run cargo publish; the tag starts the publish workflow.
• If a check fails and can't be fixed in the task, don't tag.

It runs after task 1, so the memory fix ships in the release if it lands.

